// ggufrange.nv — the type that makes this package sans-IO: a place in a
// model file, said out loud.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// THE SHAPE, AND WHY IT IS THIS ONE
//
// `gguf-nv` is a `core` package, so its budget is no effects at all: it
// cannot open a file, seek in one or map one.  A GGUF is also the one
// model format where that is a gift rather than a tax, because a GGUF
// is an INDEX — a header, a metadata table, a tensor-info table, and
// then a blob whose contents are addressed by offset — and a loader
// wants exactly one tensor at a time out of a file that does not fit in
// memory.
//
// So every answer this package gives about WHERE something is, is a
// `GgufRange`, and it means both directions at once:
//
//   - to a caller that mapped the whole file, it is a SPAN into the
//     buffer it handed over, and `ggufrange.slice` cuts it out;
//   - to a caller that mapped none of it — a loader streaming a 40 GB
//     checkpoint into VRAM a tensor at a time — it is a REQUEST: read
//     these `len` bytes at this `at` and hand them back.
//
// `docs/publishing.md` § How a `core` package takes bytes from its host
// calls the second one "the core asks, the host performs", and lists it
// as the right shape for an indexed format that streaming would throw
// away.  Both are exercised here: `gguffile.parse` is the first, and
// `ggufread.need` through `ggufinfo.tensor_range` is the second.
//
// ELF-NV'S SHAPE IS THE HOUSE STYLE, AND THE TYPE IS STILL OURS.
// `elf-nv` answers about a firmware image with an `ElfRange` for the
// same reason, and this package copies the shape deliberately so that a
// reader who has met one meets no surprise in the other.  What it does
// not do is depend on it: struct identity is keyed by name across an
// assembly, the two formats share nothing but the idea, and a model
// loader downloading an ELF reader to get a pair of integers would be
// a dependency nobody asked for.
//
// WHY NOT A SLICE TYPE THAT BORROWS
//
// Because a borrow would tie the range to the buffer it came from, and
// the interesting caller here has no buffer at all.  A range is two
// integers; it survives being written to a manifest, sent to a GPU
// upload queue, or held while the bytes it describes are on a disk.

// GgufRange is `len` bytes starting at `at`, counted from the first
// byte of the FILE.
//
// Always from the file, never from the start of the tensor data:
// `ggufinfo` adds the data-blob origin before it answers, because the
// origin is a fact the index knows and the caller should not have to.
// The tensor-info table's own `offset` field is the relative one, and
// it is kept relative on `GgufTensorInfo` so that a round trip through
// `ggufwrite` writes back what was read.
pub struct GgufRange
    // Byte offset from the start of the file.  Never negative.
    at: Int
    // Byte count.  Zero is ordinary — a metadata array of no elements
    // and a tensor with a zero dimension both occupy no bytes, and
    // their range is the empty one at the offset the table gave.
    len: Int

// range is `len` bytes at `at`.
//
// ```novo
// use ggufrange
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let r = ggufrange.range(24, 4096)
//     println("${r.len} bytes at ${r.at}")
// ```
pub fn range(at: Int, len: Int) -> GgufRange
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.range")

// empty is the zero-length range at `at`.
//
// Named rather than left to `range(at, 0)` because an empty metadata
// array is common in real checkpoints — `tokenizer.ggml.merges` on a
// SentencePiece model is the usual one — and a reader that meets one
// should see what it is.
pub fn empty(at: Int) -> GgufRange
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.empty")

// range_end is the offset one past the last byte, which is `at + len`.
pub fn range_end(r: GgufRange) -> Int
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.range_end")

// range_fits says whether the whole range is inside a file of `total`
// bytes.
//
// The check every offset field gets before it is believed.  A
// tensor-info entry is a name and four numbers a quantiser wrote, and
// nothing in the format stops it naming bytes past the end of the
// file — a truncated download is exactly that, and it is the single
// most common damaged GGUF.
pub fn range_fits(r: GgufRange, total: Int) -> Bool
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.range_fits")

// slice cuts the range out of an image the caller holds.
//
// The convenience half of the type, for the caller that DID map the
// whole file.  It copies: novo-lang has no borrowing slice, so the
// alternative to a copy is the range itself, which is why the range is
// what every other function here returns.
//
// A range that does not fit is `GgufTruncated` with all three numbers,
// rather than a short read — a caller that got fewer bytes than it
// asked for and was not told is a caller that parses garbage and blames
// the model.
pub fn slice(image: Bytes, r: GgufRange) -> Result<Bytes, GgufFault>
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.slice")

// sub is the range of `len` bytes at `offset` INSIDE `r`, expressed
// back in the file's own coordinates.
//
// The arithmetic a caller reading in ranges gets wrong once: it has the
// metadata table's bytes in a buffer of its own, it wants the third
// key's value, and the offset it must report in an error is the file's,
// not the buffer's.  Doing it here means doing it once.
pub fn sub(r: GgufRange, offset: Int, len: Int) -> Result<GgufRange, GgufFault>
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.sub")

// join is the smallest range covering both.
//
// What a loader building an upload plan wants: two tensors that happen
// to be adjacent in the file are one read, and this is the test for
// whether merging them costs anything — `join(a, b).len` against
// `a.len + b.len` is the padding that would come along.
pub fn join(left: GgufRange, right: GgufRange) -> GgufRange
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.join")

// overlaps says whether the two ranges share a byte.
//
// A tensor-info table is a list of offsets a writer chose, and nothing
// checks them against each other; two tensors claiming one byte is a
// file that will load and produce nonsense.  `ggufinfo.check_layout`
// uses this, and it is public because a tool writing a GGUF wants the
// same check before it does.
pub fn overlaps(left: GgufRange, right: GgufRange) -> Bool
    todo("gguf-nv.ggufrange.overlaps")
